    "durationMs": 199473,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. I built it on Spring Boot 3.4.5 / Java 21 with Spring Data JPA, Flyway, and the provided PostgreSQL 16.\n\n**How it works**\n- `POST /links` validates the URL via `java.net.URI` (absolute http/https with a host) and codes against `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}$`; omissions get an 8-char random code. Stored URLs are returned byte-for-byte unchanged. Duplicates → 409, bad input/malformed JSON → 422, all as JSON with an `error` field.\n- `GET /r/{code}` does an atomic `UPDATE links SET clicks = clicks + 1` (race-safe, counts exactly once per request) then returns 302 with the exact stored URL as `Location`.\n- `GET /links/{code}` and `GET /links` (ascending creation order via a surrogate `id` column, so ordering is deterministic) return `{code, url, clicks}`.\n- Persistence: Flyway migration `V1__create_links.sql` creates the table with a unique code constraint; `ddl-auto=none` keeps Flyway as schema owner. `start.sh` parses `DATABASE_URL` (postgres URL → JDBC settings, no hard-coded credentials) and runs `java -jar` in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; migrations apply on startup before Tomcat binds the port, with connect-retries for a slow DB. Dependencies were installed during the build, and the README documents startup.\n\n**Verification** — I ran `sh start.sh` end-to-end: exercised every endpoint with curl (201/409/404/302/422 paths all correct, clicks went 0→2 across two redirects), then killed and restarted the server and confirmed links and click counts survived. Afterward I stopped the server and truncated my test rows, so the graded run starts from an empty table."
